+---
+id: dl-002-json-patch-rfc-6902-semantics-for-root-path-atomicity-and-prototype-pollution
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Patch (RFC 6902) semantics for root path, atomicity, and prototype pollution"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 6902 leaves several implementation choices open, and `applyPatch` is built directly on the
+`resolvePointer`/pointer-token helpers from dl-001, so its failure modes need to be decided
+consistently with that earlier work:
+
+- The RFC defines `add`/`replace` at the root path (`""`) as replacing the whole document, but
+  doesn't say what `remove` or `move`'s `from` should do at the root, since there is no parent
+  container to remove a root value from.
+- The RFC recommends, but doesn't mandate, that a patch either applies completely or not at all.
+- `add`, `move` (destination) and `copy` (destination) can target a new object member whose name is
+  attacker-controlled, which raises the classic `__proto__` prototype-pollution hazard when that name
+  is written with bracket assignment onto a plain object.
+- `copy` duplicates a subtree into two locations of the same document.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `remove` at the root path (`""`), and `move` with `from` equal to `""`, both throw: there is no
+  container to remove the root value from, so this is treated as a failed operation rather than as
+  "replace the document with nothing" (JSON has no such value).
+- `add` and `replace` at the root path replace the whole returned document with (a clone of) `value`,
+  per RFC 6902 §4.1/§4.3.
+- `applyPatch` validates the shape of every operation in the patch array upfront (`op` is one of the
+  six names, `path` is a string, `value`/`from` are present where required) before applying any of
+  them. It then applies operations to a `structuredClone` of the input document; if any operation
+  fails mid-patch, the clone is discarded and the exception propagates, so the caller's input is
+  never mutated and never observes a partially-applied patch.
+- Every value taken from the patch (`add`/`replace`'s `value`, the value `copy` reads from `from`) is
+  `structuredClone`d before being written into the result, so the returned document shares no mutable
+  references with the patch argument or with other locations the document was copied from.
+- `add`, `replace`, `move` and `copy` all refuse a target whose last reference token is `__proto__`,
+  throwing instead of letting a bracket assignment reach the special accessor that plain objects
+  inherit from `Object.prototype`. `remove` and read-only resolution already reject `__proto__`
+  naturally, since neither one has `__proto__` as an *own* property for `hasOwnProperty` to find.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers that need "clear the document" semantics must express it explicitly (e.g. `replace` at
+  `""` with an empty object/array), rather than relying on `remove` at the root.
+- The upfront shape validation means a structurally invalid operation later in the array is caught
+  before any earlier, well-formed operation is applied — patch validity and operation failures are
+  both "all or nothing" from the caller's point of view.
+- The `__proto__` guard only blocks that one special-cased member name; it is not a general schema
+  validator for member names, and does not need to be as JSON Pointer/Patch has no other reference
+  token with special JavaScript runtime behaviour.
