@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+---
+id: task-002-implement-rfc-6902-json-patch-applypatch
+type: task
+title: "Implement RFC 6902 JSON Patch applyPatch"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Implement JSON Patch per RFC 6902, with its six operations (`add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`,
+`copy`, `test`), exported as `applyPatch(document, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It applies the patch
+to a JSON document and returns the result as a new value, without mutating its input; it throws when
+the patch is invalid or when one of its operations fails. Second feature in the series that builds on
+`resolvePointer` (task-001).
+
+## What was done
+
+- Refactored `src/jsonPointer.ts` to expose the building blocks the patch logic reuses, without
+  changing `resolvePointer`'s existing behaviour (its 15 existing tests still pass unmodified):
+  - Extracted pointer-to-tokens splitting into an exported `parsePointer`.
+  - Exported `resolveToken` (single-token resolution step) and `parseArrayIndex`.
+  - Exported `isJsonObject`.
+- Added `src/jsonPatch.ts` with `applyPatch(document, patch)`:
+  - Validates the whole patch array's shape upfront (each operation is an object with a known `op`,
+    a string `path`, `value` present for `add`/`replace`/`test`, and a string `from` for
+    `move`/`copy`) before applying anything.
+  - Works on a `structuredClone` of `document`; each operation mutates that clone in place (or, for
+    root-path `add`/`replace`/`move`, replaces it outright), so the original `document` is never
+    touched and a mid-patch failure leaves no partial result observable.
+  - `add`: supports object members, in-bounds array insertion, and the `-` append token; requires the
+    parent to already exist.
+  - `remove`: requires the target member/element to exist; throws at the root path (no container to
+    remove the root from).
+  - `replace`: requires the target to already exist; supports the root path (replaces the whole
+    document).
+  - `move`: removes from `from` then adds at `path`, re-resolving `path`'s parent after the removal
+    so within-array shifts match the RFC's worked example; rejects `from` being a proper prefix of
+    `path`; throws when `from` is the root.
+  - `copy`: deep-clones the value read from `from` before adding it at `path`, so the two locations
+    never alias.
+  - `test`: RFC-6902-style deep equality (object member order ignored, array order significant).
+  - `add`/`replace`/`move`/`copy` refuse a target whose last token is `__proto__`, to avoid writing
+    through the inherited prototype accessor of a plain object; `remove` and pointer resolution are
+    already safe since `__proto__` is never an own property.
+  - Values taken from the patch (`value`, and the subtree `copy` reads) are `structuredClone`d before
+    being written into the result, so the output shares no mutable references with the patch input.
+- `src/index.ts` now also re-exports `applyPatch` and the `JsonPatchOperation` type.
+- Added `src/jsonPatch.test.ts` (37 tests, written before the implementation: red, then green),
+  covering each operation's happy path, its documented edge/error cases, RFC 6902 Appendix A examples
+  (object/array add, array move with index shift, nested add), no-mutation-of-input, atomicity on
+  mid-patch failure, malformed-patch rejection, and the `__proto__` guard.
+- Recorded the root-path, atomicity, and prototype-pollution design choices as
+  dl-002-json-patch-rfc-6902-semantics-for-root-path-atomicity-and-prototype-pollution.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 52/52 passing (15 pre-existing `jsonPointer` tests unchanged + 37 new `jsonPatch`
+  tests).
+- `npm run typecheck` — no errors.
